WEBVTT

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:18.262
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> Johannes Vermeer was a seventeenth-century painter

00:00:18.561 --> 00:00:20.528
from the city of Delft in Holland.

00:00:21.501 --> 00:00:25.256
He mainly painted the people and things he saw around him:

00:00:25.695 --> 00:00:31.173
the rooms in his house, the people who lived or worked there (usually women),

00:00:31.737 --> 00:00:33.810
and the things they did every day.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:39.660
For example, in his work you will see women who are playing music,

00:00:39.984 --> 00:00:43.893
reading or writing letters, or working in the kitchen.

00:00:45.015 --> 00:00:50.455
Vermeer was especially good at painting light coming into a room through windows.

00:00:51.607 --> 00:00:58.494
Partly for this reason, people often describe his work as being like photography or film –

00:00:59.118 --> 00:01:01.699
his paintings can seem very ‘real’.

00:01:03.138 --> 00:01:08.534
This painting, <i>The Milkmaid</i>, is one of these very ‘photographic’ images.

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:14.345
The woman, a maid or a servant, is pouring milk into a bowl.

00:01:15.165 --> 00:01:17.928
Perhaps she’s making a bread and milk pudding,

00:01:18.369 --> 00:01:21.460
because there are pieces of broken bread on the table.

00:01:22.954 --> 00:01:28.031
Nobody knows if the woman he painted was a real servant or a model.

00:01:28.618 --> 00:01:34.935
However, most people think Vermeer usually painted his wife, his daughter,

00:01:35.151 --> 00:01:37.788
and his servant, not models.

00:01:39.047 --> 00:01:43.685
A famous book and film, called <i>Girl with a Pearl Earring</i>,

00:01:44.052 --> 00:01:49.242
inspired by one of his best-known paintings, is an imaginary story

00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:53.178
about Vermeer and his relationship with his young servant.

00:01:54.620 --> 00:02:00.201
People admired Vermeer’s paintings a lot in his lifetime, but he was never rich.

00:02:01.396 --> 00:02:03.235
There are two reasons for this.

00:02:03.744 --> 00:02:06.920
First, because he painted very slowly.

00:02:07.679 --> 00:02:13.661
Today there are only about thirty-four paintings which we can be sure are by him.

00:02:14.659 --> 00:02:18.452
Second, because he used very expensive paints.

00:02:19.611 --> 00:02:24.801
The blue paint he used for the milkmaid’s apron was made of lapis lazuli,

00:02:25.041 --> 00:02:27.227
which was a very expensive stone.

00:02:28.695 --> 00:02:32.915
People loved this painting from the very beginning, and although

00:02:32.915 --> 00:02:37.730
it is very small, only forty-six by forty-one centimetres,

00:02:38.515 --> 00:02:42.302
twenty years after Vermeer died, somebody bought the

00:02:42.302 --> 00:02:45.931
painting for a hundred and seventy-five Dutch guilders.

00:02:46.744 --> 00:02:49.716
That was an enormous amount of money for the time.